
How They Fit Together (The Compilation Cycle)
The Preprocessor (#include): When you compile, the #include "log.h" command basically copies and pastes the contents of log.h right into the top of main.cpp and log.cpp. This satisfies the compiler by proving that the functions are declared.

The Compiler: The compiler looks at main.cpp and log.cpp independently, translating them into object files (main.o and log.o).

The Linker (g++ main.cpp log.cpp): This is the crucial final step. The Linker takes main.o (which calls the functions) and log.o (which contains the functions) and stitches them together into your final executable (main.exe).

If you forget to compile log.cpp alongside main.cpp, the compiler gets through the first two steps fine, but the Linker panics because it has a call to Log() in main with no physical implementation to point to—which results in that undefined reference error you saw earlier!






In C++, std::cin.get(); pauses the program and waits for the user to press the Enter key before it continues or closes.

Why is it used?
When you run a C++ program from an IDE or by double-clicking the executable file, the program opens a command prompt window, executes all your code in a split second, and then instantly closes.

Because it closes immediately, you wouldn't normally have time to see your output (like "Hello World!") printed on the screen.

By putting std::cin.get(); at the end of your main() function:

The program hits that line and pauses.

It waits for keyboard input (specifically, waiting for you to press Enter).

Once you press Enter, the program finishes and safely closes the console window.

(Note: If you are running your program straight from an advanced terminal like PowerShell where the window stays open automatically after the program finishes, you technically don't need it, but it is a common helper line used in beginner tutorials so the console window doesn't vanish on you).